sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2015-girardia-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The authors suggest that a core network of gene-regulatory elements and gap-junction-mediated signals may be shared among many planarians, with species differences tweaking its dynamics, so that evolutionary distance between planarian species may reflect how easily this network can be shifted into its different outcome states, as during gap junction inhibition.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Planarian evolutionary distance may reflect ease of shifting a shared patterning network";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Emmons-Bell M, Durant F, Hammelman J, Bessonov N, Volpert V, Morokuma J, Pinet K, Adams DS, Pietak A, Lobo D, Levin M, “Gap Junctional Blockade Stochastically Induces Different Species-Specific Head Anatomies in Genetically Wild-Type Girardia dorotocephala Flatworms”, International journal of molecular sciences (2015), doi:10.3390/ijms161126065; section “3. Discussion › 3.8. Stochastic Phenotypes and Evolutionary Implications”. Scope: Planarian species (Girardia, Dugesia, Schmidtea, Polycelis/Phagocata)"
}
